>:<fpage>11430</fpage>&#x02013;11435.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17592133</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Kawana, K., A.J. Quayle, M. Ficarra, J.A. Ibana, L. Shen, Y. Kawana, H. Yang, L. Marrero, S. Yavagal, S.J. Greene, et al. <year>2007</year>. CD1d degradation in <italic>Chlamydia trachomatis</italic>-infected epithelial cells is the result of both cellular and chlamydial proteasomal activity. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>282</volume>:<fpage>7368</fpage>&#x02013;7375.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17215251</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Lad, S.P., G. Yang, D.A. Scott, G. Wang, P. Nair, J. Mathison, V.S. Reddy, and E. Li. <year>2007</year>. Chlamydial CT441 is a PDZ domain-containing tail-specific protease that interferes with the NF-kappaB pathway of immune response. <source>J. Bacteriol.</source> <volume>189</volume>:<fpage>6619</fpage>&#x02013;6625.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17631635</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Lockshin, R.A., and Z. Zakeri. <year>2004</year>. Caspase-independent cell death? <source>Oncogene.</source> <volume>23</volume>:<fpage>2766</fpage>&#x02013;2773.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15077140</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">McClarty, G. <year>1994</year>. Chlamydiae and the biochemistry of intracellular parasitism. <source>Trends Microbiol.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>157</fpage>&#x02013;164.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8055179</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Nicholson, D.W., A. Ali, N.A. Thornberry, J.P. Vaillancourt, C.K. Ding, M. Gallant, Y. Gareau, P.R. Griffin, M. Labelle, Y.A. Lazebnik, et al. <year>1995</year>. Identification and inhibition of the ICE/CED-3 protease necessary for mammalian apoptosis. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>376</volume>:<fpage>37</fpage>&#x02013;43.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7596430</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Ojcius, D.M., P. Souque, J.L. Perfettini, and A. Dautry-Varsat. <year>1998</year>. Apoptosis of epithelial cells and macrophages due to infection with the obligate intracellular pathogen <italic>Chlamydia psittaci</italic>. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>161</volume>:<fpage>4220</fpage>&#x02013;4226.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9780196</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Paetzel, M., and R.E. Dalbey. <year>1997</year>. Catalytic hydroxyl/amine dyads within serine proteases. <source>Trends Biochem. Sci.</source> <volume>22</volume>:<fpage>28</fpage>&#x02013;31.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9020589</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Perfettini, J.L., J.C. Reed, N. Israel, J.C. Martinou, A. Dautry-Varsat, and D.M. Ojcius. <year>2002</year>. Role of Bcl-2 family members in caspase-independent apoptosis during <italic>Chlamydia</italic> infection. <source>Infect. Immun.</source> <volume>70</volume>:<fpage>55</fpage>&#x02013;61.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11748163</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Peters, J., D.P. Wilson, G. Myers, P. Timms, and P.M. Bavoil. <year>2007</year>. Type III secretion a la <italic>Chlamydia</italic>. <source>Trends Microbiol.</source> <volume>15</volume>:<fpage>241</fpage>&#x02013;251.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17482820</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Pirbhai, M., F. Dong, Y. Zhong, K.Z. Pan, and G. Zhong. <year>2006</year>. The secreted protease factor CPAF is responsible for degrading pro-apoptotic BH3-only proteins in <italic>Chlamydia</italic> trachomatis-infected cells. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>281</volume>:<fpage>31495</fpage>&#x02013;31501.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16940052</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Pop, C., J. Timmer, S. Sperandio, and G.S. Salv